      <div class="badge-row">
        <span class="badge purple">💼 HR &amp; Onboarding</span>
        <span class="badge blue">🎉 Team Building</span>
        <span class="badge">⏱ 11 min read</span>
      </div>

      <h1>
        Spin Wheel <em>Icebreakers</em> for Onboarding — A 2026 Playbook for HR
        Teams
      </h1>

      <p class="hero-intro">
        A new hire's first meeting is the one everyone in the room has done a
        hundred times and the new person has done zero times. This is a working
        playbook — not a listicle — for closing that gap with a spin wheel: six
        wheel setups you can copy directly, a short script for running the room,
        and the specific ways this exercise goes wrong.
      </p>

      <figure class="hero-image">
        <img
          src="/eng-team-picker-wheel.webp"
          alt="Illustration of a spin wheel used during a new employee onboarding session, with a small team gathered around a laptop"
          width="1400"
          height="700"
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>
          A spin wheel gives a first-day introduction a shared, visible focal
          point instead of an awkward go-around.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="meta-row">
        <span>✅ Updated November 24, 2026</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>📂 HR &amp; Onboarding</span>
      </div>

      <div class="info-box purple">
        <div class="info-box-label">💡 New to spin wheels?</div>
        <p>
          If you haven't used one of these before, start with
          <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-the-wheel-tool"
            >What Is a Spin the Wheel Tool and How Does It Work?</a
          >
          for the short version of how the randomization works and how to build
          your first wheel. Then come back here for the onboarding- specific
          setups.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ToC -->
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <div class="toc-title">📋 In This Guide</div>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <a href="#problem">1. What Actually Goes Wrong in Day-One Intros</a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="#why-wheel"
              >2. Why Handing the Decision to a Wheel Helps</a
            >
