      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <h2 id="problem">1. What Actually Goes Wrong in Day-One Intros</h2>

      <p>
        Watch a typical first-day round of introductions and you'll notice the
        same failure pattern almost every time. The facilitator says "let's go
        around and share something fun about yourself," and there's a beat of
        silence before the first brave (or nearest) person starts. By the third
        or fourth person, the answers have collapsed into the same three
        templates — a hobby, a pet, a hometown — because everyone is quietly
        copying the format set by whoever went first.
      </p>

      <p>
        The new hire, meanwhile, is doing three things at once: trying to
        remember a dozen new names, trying to read the room's sense of humor,
        and trying to figure out what "fun fact" even means in a context where
        they don't yet know what's considered normal here. That's a lot of
        cognitive load for one exercise that's supposed to be relaxing.
      </p>

      <p>
        There's also a quieter problem: who picks who goes next. If a manager
        points at people, it can feel like being called on in class. If it's
        left open, the same two or three confident people jump in every time and
        the quiet ones — often including the new hire — never get pulled in.
        Neither pattern is anyone's fault. It's just what happens when a human
        has to make a selection decision in real time in front of the group.
      </p>

      <p>
        A spin wheel doesn't fix the content problem on its own — you still need
        decent questions — but it removes the selection problem entirely, and it
        breaks the copy-the-previous-answer pattern because each spin can land
        on a genuinely different prompt.
      </p>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <h2 id="why-wheel">2. Why Handing the Decision to a Wheel Helps</h2>

      <p>
        The mechanism is simple and it shows up in plenty of group settings
        beyond onboarding — the same logic is behind why
        <a
          href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-wheel-classroom-activities"
          >teachers use it to call on students</a
        >
        and why
        <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/spin-wheel-team-building"
          >remote teams use it for lightweight decisions</a
        >. When the outcome comes from a visibly neutral process, people accept
        it faster and with less second-guessing than when a person chooses.
      </p>

      <div class="feature-grid">
        <div class="feature-card">
          <div class="feature-icon">🎭</div>
          <div class="feature-num">Reason 01</div>
          <h3>No one is "called on"</h3>
          <p>
            The wheel picks, not the facilitator — which matters most for a new
            hire who doesn't yet know the room's social rules.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
          <div class="feature-icon">🎲</div>
          <div class="feature-num">Reason 02</div>
          <h3>Breaks the copy pattern</h3>
          <p>
            Going in a fixed order lets everyone anchor on the first answer. A
            random spin means the next question can be unrelated to the last
            one.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
          <div class="feature-icon">⏱</div>
          <div class="feature-num">Reason 03</div>
          <h3>Naturally paces the room</h3>
          <p>
            One spin, one question, one answer. It's a built-in timer that stops
            any single person from running long.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-card">
          <div class="feature-icon">🔁</div>
          <div class="feature-num">Reason 04</div>
          <h3>Reusable with zero rebuild</h3>
          <p>
            Build the wheel once, bookmark the link, and every future new hire
            gets the same setup with no prep required.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        For the underlying research on why people trust and cooperate with
        randomized processes more readily than human-made ones, see our piece on
        <a
          href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/psychology-random-decision-making"
          >the psychology behind random decision making</a
        >
        — it's written for group facilitation generally, but every point in it
        maps directly onto a first-day intro round.
      </p>

