      <h2 id="wheels">3. Six Wheel Setups You Can Copy Right Now</h2>

      <p>
        These are ready to paste into a wheel on
        <a href="https://spinthewheelsonline.com/">spinthewheelsonline.com</a>.
        Each one is built for a different moment in the onboarding timeline —
        pick the one that matches where your new hire actually is, not
        necessarily the first one on the list.
      </p>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 1 — First-Day Question Spinner</h3>
      <p>
        One wheel, one spin per person. Turn on
        <strong>"remove after spin"</strong> so no question repeats during the
        session.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>What's a job you had before this one that nobody would guess?</li>
          <li>What's a small thing that instantly puts you in a good mood?</li>
          <li>What's something you're weirdly good at outside of work?</li>
          <li>What's the last thing you got way too into?</li>
          <li>
            If you had a free Tuesday, what would you actually do with it?
          </li>
          <li>What's a place you'd go back to in a heartbeat?</li>
          <li>What's on your desk or desktop that says something about you?</li>
          <li>What's a skill you're currently trying to pick up?</li>
          <li>Dogs, cats, or "I have opinions about neither"?</li>
          <li>
            What's a show or podcast you've told three people to watch/listen to
            this year?
          </li>
          <li>What's your go-to order at a coffee shop?</li>
          <li>What's something you learned recently that surprised you?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 2 — Structured Self-Intro Prompts</h3>
      <p>
        This wheel doesn't pick a question — it picks a format for each person's
        30-second intro, so everyone gives roughly the same amount of
        information without it sounding scripted.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>Name, role, and how you actually spend your weekends</li>
          <li>Name, role, and where you grew up</li>
          <li>Name, role, and what you wanted to be as a kid</li>
          <li>Name, role, and the last thing you binged</li>
          <li>Name, role, and your first-ever job</li>
          <li>Name, role, and something most people don't know about you</li>
          <li>Name, role, and what made you laugh recently</li>
          <li>Name, role, and one thing you're learning right now</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 3 — Category Picker for "Two Truths, One Lie"</h3>
      <p>
        Everyone prepares two truths and a lie, but the wheel decides which
        category they have to draw from — which stops people from defaulting to
        the same safe answer every time. Works for groups of four to twenty.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>Travel</li>
          <li>Childhood</li>
          <li>Work &amp; career</li>
          <li>Hobbies &amp; skills</li>
          <li>Food</li>
          <li>Brushes with fame</li>
          <li>Awards or achievements</li>
          <li>Odd fears or pet peeves</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box green">
        <div class="info-box-label">✅ Facilitator tip</div>
        <p>
          Give everyone sixty seconds to think after the category lands, before
          anyone shares. Without a pause, whoever's up first is improvising
          while everyone else gets prep time — which isn't fair and usually
          shows.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 4 — Simultaneous "This or That" Round</h3>
      <p>
        Best for larger groups. Spin, everyone answers at once (thumbs up or
        down on a call, raised hands in a room), no individual prep needed. New
        hires learn how the team actually works faster than any handbook would
        tell them.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>Camera on or camera off?</li>
          <li>Written brief or a quick call?</li>
          <li>Reply right away or batch messages later?</li>
          <li>Plan the day or wing it?</li>
          <li>Meetings in the morning or afternoon?</li>
          <li>Work from a café or from home?</li>
          <li>Deep focus blocks or constant check-ins?</li>
          <li>Big team energy or small-group energy?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 5 — "Ask an Expert" Finder</h3>
      <p>
        Put every current team member's name on one wheel and a set of topics on
        a second. Spin both — that person shares a real tip or story on that
        topic. This is the one that actually surfaces the knowledge that usually
        only lives in people's heads.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Topic Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>The shortcut nobody tells new people about</li>
          <li>A mistake you learned the most from</li>
          <li>A tool the team underuses</li>
          <li>Something you wish someone told you in week one</li>
          <li>An unwritten rule that actually matters here</li>
          <li>Who helped you most when you were new</li>
          <li>The strangest request you've ever gotten from a client</li>
          <li>An underrated resource most people skip</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>🎡 Wheel 6 — First-Week Wrap-Up Draw</h3>
      <p>
        Run this at the end of week one, and let the new hire spin it
        themselves. After a week of being told what to do, handing them the spin
        for something good is a small but real shift in who's in control.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">
          Wheel Entries <span class="wheel-tag">Copy + Paste</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="wheel-entries">
          <li>Team lunch — you pick the place</li>
          <li>Leave an hour early on Friday</li>
          <li>15 minutes with the founder or a senior leader</li>
          <li>Pick next week's team activity</li>
          <li>A small learning-budget top-up</li>
          <li>Run a 10-minute "teach us something" session</li>
          <li>Work from wherever you want for a day</li>
          <li>Team trivia or a short game session</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

