      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <h2 id="remote">5. Running It on a Video Call</h2>
      <p>
        Remote onboarding has one problem in-person doesn't: the new hire is
        meeting a grid of faces that already have history with each other, and
        they can't read any of the physical-room cues that normally fill in the
        gaps. A shared screen with a wheel on it gives the call something to
        look at besides a face — which takes real pressure off everyone.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Load the wheel before the call starts.</strong> Nothing kills
          momentum like building it live while people watch.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Screen-share the tab</strong> the moment intros start — Zoom,
          Teams, and Meet all pass the spin animation through cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Share desktop audio if your platform allows it.</strong> The
          tick as the wheel slows adds real suspense even through a screen.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turn on "remove after spin"</strong> for anything where each
          person or question should only come up once.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bookmark the wheel's URL.</strong> The setup lives in the
          link, so the next session takes zero rebuild time.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="info-box blue">
        <div class="info-box-label">🌍 Hybrid sessions</div>
        <p>
          If some people are in the room and some are remote, project the wheel
          on the room's screen <em>and</em> share it on the call at the same
          time. Both groups watching the same spin land is a small detail, but
          it's the difference between "the remote people joined" and everyone
          actually being in the same activity.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <h2 id="script">6. A Facilitator's Script (What to Actually Say)</h2>
      <p>
        The wheel does the picking, but someone still has to open and close the
        exercise. Here's a script you can adapt rather than winging it cold.
      </p>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">🎙 Opening</div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-soft); margin-bottom: 0">
          "We're going to do a quick round with this wheel instead of going in
          order — it'll land on someone at random, they answer whatever question
          it picks, and then we spin again. If you land on something you'd
          rather skip, there's a pass option — just say pass and we'll spin
          again, no explanation needed."
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">🎙 During</div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-soft); margin-bottom: 0">
          Spin, read the result out loud, and give the person a beat before they
          have to answer — even two seconds of silence feels longer than it is.
          If the room's low-energy, answer a question yourself first to reset
          the tone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="wheel-box">
        <div class="wheel-box-title">🎙 Closing</div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-soft); margin-bottom: 0">
          "That's everyone — thanks for playing along. [New hire], anything you
          heard that you want to follow up on later? That's usually a better way
          to build on this than anything I could plan."
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 7 -->
      <h2 id="mistakes">7. The Mistakes That Quietly Kill It</h2>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(52, 211, 153, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">✅</div>
          <h3>Do: state the "no re-spins" rule upfront</h3>
          <p>
            Saying it once, before the first spin, prevents anyone from quietly
            lobbying for a redo later.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(52, 211, 153, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">✅</div>
          <h3>Do: let the new hire go first</h3>
          <p>
            Handing them the first spin signals the team is genuinely making
            room, not just going through motions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(52, 211, 153, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">✅</div>
          <h3>Do: build in a pass option</h3>
          <p>
            One "pass, spin again" entry respects whatever reason someone has
            for skipping, without needing to know it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">❌</div>
          <h3>Don't: reach for personal topics</h3>
          <p>
            Family, health, religion, and relationships create discomfort in a
            professional first meeting — keep it light.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">❌</div>
          <h3>Don't: run it past 15 minutes</h3>
          <p>
            The exercise that people enjoy at ten minutes is the one people
            dread at twenty-five. Time-box it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="feature-card"
          style="border-color: rgba(248, 113, 113, 0.25)"
        >
          <div class="feature-icon">❌</div>
          <h3>Don't: skip it for senior hires</h3>
          <p>
            Experienced hires get left out of icebreakers under the assumption
            they don't need one — they usually enjoy it more, not less.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

