      <h2 id="measuring">9. How to Tell If It's Actually Working</h2>
      <p>
        You don't need a survey to know whether this is landing. A few informal
        signals are usually enough:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          People are still talking to each other after the wheel exercise ends,
          unprompted.
        </li>
        <li>
          The new hire references something from the round later that week — a
          name, a joke, a shared interest.
        </li>
        <li>
          Veterans engage instead of visibly waiting for their turn to be over.
        </li>
        <li>
          The next new hire's session doesn't need you to explain the format
          twice.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If none of that is happening after a couple of sessions, the wheel
        probably isn't the problem — it's usually the questions being too
        generic, the group being too large for the format, or the exercise
        running too long. Adjust those before dropping the tool altogether.
      </p>

